.TH XSF 1M "15 November 2026" "xsurf"
.SH NAME
xsf \- the state of the X-Surf and X-Surf-100 ethernet boards
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B xsf
.RI [ unit ]
.br
.B "xsf -a"
.br
.B "xsf -S"
.RI [ unit ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B xsf
shows what the
.BR xsurf (7)
driver knows about board
.I unit
(0 when not given), that is interface
.BI xsf unit\fR.
It first prints the line the driver printed on the console at load, which
is gone once the system is up:
.PP
.RS
.ft CW
.nf
xsf0: X-Surf-100, Zorro 3 at 0x40000000, 00:80:10:12:34:56, interrupt I/O, link up 100 full
.fi
.ft R
.RE
.PP
with the link as it is now. Then, indented, the frames received and sent
with their errors and collisions, the transmit failures by kind (not
completed, remote-DMA write not completed, aborted), what they point at
when no frame was ever sent, and which failures were said on the console.
Then what the driver read when it probed the board: the chip's command
and interrupt registers before its reset, how long it took to become
ready after it, what the register check read back by each kind of cycle,
the station-address PROM as read, the remote-DMA self-test's result, and
the chip's registers at the first transmit timeout and the first
remote-DMA write timeout. These lines are what to send when the board
does not work.
Then every counter of the driver's status record, one per line as
.IR "name value" ,
for scripts and for reports.
.PP
After a board in use, and when
.BI xsf unit
does not exist,
.B xsf
lists each board the driver found but refused at load, with the reason
its console line gave:
.PP
.RS
.ft CW
.nf
board 0: X-Surf-100, Zorro 3 at 0x40000000: refused at load: its registers do not take writes (wrote 0x55 or 0xaa, read 0x99)
.fi
.ft R
.RE
.TP
.B \-a
every board the driver found, in use or refused, in the order it probed
them.
.TP
.B \-S
silent: exit status 0 when
.BI xsf unit
is in use, 1 when it is not.
.SH READING IT
.TP
.B "no link"
the cable, the hub or switch port, or the card's PHY. Look at the link
lights of the card and of the port.
.TP
.B "refused at load: its registers do not take writes"
what the driver writes to the chip's registers does not come back: the
fault lies between the Amiga and the chip: the card, its slot, the bus or the
driver; the readings below the counters say which step failed. Not the network.
.TP
.B "no frame reached the chip: its remote-DMA writes do not complete"
frames written into the chip's memory never complete: the fault lies
between the Amiga and the chip: the card, its slot, the bus or the
driver; the readings below the counters say which step failed.
.TP
.B "no transmit has completed"
the chip takes frames but never reports them sent or failed. The chips'
transmitters do not wait for a link (their datasheets), so a missing link
alone does not explain it: the fault lies between the Amiga and the chip: the card, its slot, the bus or the
driver; the readings below the counters say which step failed.
.TP
.B "every transmit aborted on collisions"
the medium. On the X-Surf, a 10BASE-T link test that fails makes the chip
send on its coax port; with nothing on it every frame collides.
.TP
.B "polled (interrupt self-test failed)"
the board's interrupt did not arrive at load; the driver polls instead and
the network still works.
.PP
The counters' names are the status record's fields
.RB ( xsurfuser.h
in the driver's sources): the
.B tx_
and
.B rx_
counters, the interrupts taken
.RB ( irq_claims ),
the chip restarts
.RB ( resets ),
and the link as numbers
.RB ( link ": 0 down, 1 up, 2 not known;"
.BR link_raw :
the register the chip reports it in).
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /dev/xsf0, /dev/xsf1
the interfaces.
.TP
.B /dev/xsfctl
the driver's status node: every board found, also refused ones.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status 0 when a board in use was shown, 1 when
.BI xsf unit
is not in use or the driver is not loaded, 2 for a usage error.
.TP
.B "xsf: the xsurf driver is not loaded"
AutoConfig showed neither card at boot, so the module was not loaded.
.TP
.B "xsf: no /dev/xsfctl"
the xsurf package is older than 1.0.0-4: only the counters of a board in
use can be shown, without words.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xsurf (7),
.BR ifconfig (1M),
.BR netstat (1M)
